<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anna of Kiev</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1030–after 1075</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Anne of Kiev · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rurik-dynasty">Rurik dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Anna of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> was a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-the-wise">Yaroslav the Wise</a> who became queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> as the wife of Henry I, whom she married in 1051. She was the mother of Philip I and acted with him in government during his minority after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator">Henry</a>'s death in 1060. Her later marriage to Count Ralph of Valois caused a scandal. Her marriage is a sign of the standing of the Rurikid dynasty in eleventh-century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anna-of-kiev.jpg" alt="Anna of Kiev" width="240" height="502" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AnnaKyjev.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Anne was a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslav-the-wise">Yaroslav the Wise</a>, Grand Prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a>, and his second wife Ingegerd Olofsdotter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>. Her birth date is unknown: Philippe Delorme has suggested 1027, and Andrew Gregorovich 1032. She was almost certainly the youngest daughter. A Cyrillic signature on a document of 1061 suggests that she could write her name. Yaroslav had married several of his children to Western rulers in an attempt to avoid Byzantine influence.</p><p>Negotiations for her marriage to King Henry I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> began in the late 1040s, after the death of his first wife and their only child. Henry needed an heir, and the Church increasingly disapproved of marriages between relatives, so he sought an unrelated bride. He sent Bishop Gauthier of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meaux">Meaux</a> and others to Yaroslav's court, and no record of the dowry survives. Anne travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a> in 1050.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Anne married Henry, who was nearly twenty years older, on 19 May 1051, and was crowned immediately afterwards, the first French queen to celebrate her coronation at Reims Cathedral. They had three sons, Philip, Robert, who died young, and Hugh. Anne is often credited with introducing the Greek name Philip into the royal houses of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-europe">Western Europe</a>. Few records show her taking part in the royal council, but a charter of 1058 grants a privilege with her approval and that of her children.</p><p>Henry died on 4 August 1060 and Philip succeeded him. Count Baldwin V of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> became his guardian, but Anne's name appears in four times as many charters as Baldwin's. Her only surviving signature, in Cyrillic, probably reading Ana Reina, dates from this time.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Evidence of Anne's role in government disappears in 1061, around the time she married Count Ralph IV of Valois. The marriage was controversial because Ralph was Henry's cousin and was still technically married to his second wife, and he was excommunicated. Ralph died in 1074. In 1062 Anne paid for the restoration of a chapel at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senlis">Senlis</a>, which became the Abbey of St. Vincent. The date of her death is unknown: Delorme believes it was 5 September 1075, and others propose 1080.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Anne of Kiev” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…royal houses of France, Norway, Hungary and Byzantium, a sign of the dynasty's standing in Europe; his daughter <b>Anna of Kiev</b> became queen of France as the wife of Henry I. The conversion of Rus' fixed the religious and cultural divide between Orthodox eastern Europe and the Latin…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus' · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>King Henry I of France, seeking a third wife further afield, married <b>Anna of Kiev</b>, a daughter of Yaroslav the Wise of Kievan Rus'.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-19">19 May in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kyiv</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.45,30.52&amp;year=1059" aria-label="Kyiv on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5247 411 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Anna of Kiev"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5697 681h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5697 681h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.45,30.52&amp;year=1059">What was here in 1059? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
